1
00:00:08,680 --> 00:00:12,863
Everyone who has travelled over Eastern England knows the smaller country-houses

2
00:00:12,863 --> 00:00:15,896
with which it is studded—the rather dank little buildings,

3
00:00:15,896 --> 00:00:20,026
usually in the Italian style, surrounded with parks of some eighty to a hundred

4
00:00:20,026 --> 00:00:20,626
acres.

5
00:00:21,280 --> 00:00:26,131
For me they have always had a very strong attraction: with the grey paling of split

6
00:00:26,131 --> 00:00:31,040
oak, the noble trees, the meres with their reed-beds, and the line of distant woods.

7
00:00:32,220 --> 00:00:36,742
Then, I like the pillared portico—perhaps stuck on to a red-brick Queen Anne house

8
00:00:36,742 --> 00:00:41,264
which has been faced with stucco to bring it into line with the “Grecian” taste of

9
00:00:41,264 --> 00:00:45,290
the end of the eighteenth century; the hall inside, going up to the roof,

10
00:00:45,290 --> 00:00:49,260
which hall ought always to be provided with a gallery and a small organ.

11
00:00:50,060 --> 00:00:53,526
I like the library, too, where you may find anything from a Psalter of the

12
00:00:53,526 --> 00:00:55,540
thirteenth century to a Shakespeare quarto.

13
00:00:56,360 --> 00:01:00,998
I like the pictures, of course; and perhaps most of all I like fancying what life in

14
00:01:00,998 --> 00:01:03,262
such a house was when it was first built,

15
00:01:03,262 --> 00:01:07,348
and in the piping times of landlords’ prosperity, and not least now, when,

16
00:01:07,348 --> 00:01:11,820
if money is not so plentiful, taste is more varied and life quite as interesting.

17
00:01:12,720 --> 00:01:14,451
I wish to have one of these houses,

18
00:01:14,451 --> 00:01:18,260
and enough money to keep it together and entertain my friends in it modestly.

19
00:01:19,060 --> 00:01:20,400
But this is a digression.

20
00:01:20,960 --> 00:01:24,901
I have to tell you of a curious series of events which happened in such a house as I

21
00:01:24,901 --> 00:01:25,980
have tried to describe.

22
00:01:26,940 --> 00:01:29,040
It is Castringham Hall in Suffolk.

23
00:01:29,940 --> 00:01:34,739
I think a good deal has been done to the building since the period of my story,

24
00:01:34,739 --> 00:01:39,295
but the essential features I have sketched are still there—Italian portico,

25
00:01:39,295 --> 00:01:44,033
square block of white house, older inside than out, park with fringe of woods,

26
00:01:44,033 --> 00:01:44,580
and mere.

27
00:01:44,580 --> 00:01:47,880
The one feature that marked out the house from a score of others is gone.

28
00:01:48,800 --> 00:01:53,120
As you looked at it from the park, you saw on the right a great old ash-tree growing

29
00:01:53,120 --> 00:01:55,074
within half a dozen yards of the wall,

30
00:01:55,074 --> 00:01:58,160
and almost or quite touching the building with its branches.

31
00:01:59,120 --> 00:02:03,392
I suppose it had stood there ever since Castringham ceased to be a fortified place,

32
00:02:03,392 --> 00:02:07,200
and since the moat was filled in and the Elizabethan dwelling-house built.

33
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:12,560
At any rate, it had well-nigh attained its full dimensions in the year 1690.

34
00:02:13,320 --> 00:02:17,286
In that year the district in which the Hall is situated was the scene of a number of

35
00:02:17,286 --> 00:02:17,900
witch-trials.

36
00:02:18,860 --> 00:02:23,305
It will be long, I think, before we arrive at a just estimate of the amount of solid

37
00:02:23,305 --> 00:02:27,591
reason—if there was any—which lay at the root of the universal fear of witches in

38
00:02:27,591 --> 00:02:28,191
old times.

39
00:02:29,020 --> 00:02:32,965
Whether the persons accused of this offence really did imagine that they were

40
00:02:32,965 --> 00:02:36,962
possessed of unusual power of any kind; or whether they had the will at least,

41
00:02:36,962 --> 00:02:39,832
if not the power, of doing mischief to their neighbours;

42
00:02:39,832 --> 00:02:42,855
or whether all the confessions, of which there are so many,

43
00:02:42,855 --> 00:02:47,159
were extorted by the mere cruelty of the witch-finders—these are questions which are

44
00:02:47,159 --> 00:02:48,440
not, I fancy, yet solved.

45
00:02:49,240 --> 00:02:51,160
And the present narrative gives me pause.

46
00:02:52,220 --> 00:02:54,760
I cannot altogether sweep it away as mere invention.

47
00:02:55,900 --> 00:02:58,540
The reader must judge for himself.

48
00:02:59,100 --> 00:03:02,180
Castringham contributed a victim to the auto-da-fé.

49
00:03:02,180 --> 00:03:06,554
Mrs Mothersole was her name, and she differed from the ordinary run of village

50
00:03:06,554 --> 00:03:10,760
witches only in being rather better off and in a more influential position.

51
00:03:11,760 --> 00:03:15,180
Efforts were made to save her by several reputable farmers of the parish.

52
00:03:16,200 --> 00:03:18,818
They did their best to testify to her character,

53
00:03:18,818 --> 00:03:22,200
and showed considerable anxiety as to the verdict of the jury.

54
00:03:22,920 --> 00:03:26,859
But what seems to have been fatal to the woman was the evidence of the then

55
00:03:26,859 --> 00:03:29,380
proprietor of Castringham Hall—Sir Matthew Fell.

56
00:03:30,320 --> 00:03:34,028
He deposed to having watched her on three different occasions from his window,

57
00:03:34,028 --> 00:03:37,640
at the full of the moon, gathering sprigs “from the ash-tree near my house”.

58
00:03:38,320 --> 00:03:41,555
She had climbed into the branches, clad only in her shift,

59
00:03:41,555 --> 00:03:45,068
and was cutting off small twigs with a peculiarly curved knife,

60
00:03:45,068 --> 00:03:48,080
and as she did so she seemed to be talking to herself.

61
00:03:48,940 --> 00:03:52,131
On each occasion Sir Matthew had done his best to capture the woman,

62
00:03:52,131 --> 00:03:55,322
but she had always taken alarm at some accidental noise he had made,

63
00:03:55,322 --> 00:03:59,124
and all he could see when he got down to the garden was a hare running across the

64
00:03:59,124 --> 00:04:00,860
path in the direction of the village.

65
00:04:01,940 --> 00:04:05,723
On the third night he had been at the pains to follow at his best speed,

66
00:04:05,723 --> 00:04:08,245
and had gone straight to Mrs Mothersole’s house;

67
00:04:08,245 --> 00:04:11,712
but he had had to wait a quarter of an hour battering at her door,

68
00:04:11,712 --> 00:04:15,127
and then she had come out very cross, and apparently very sleepy,

69
00:04:15,127 --> 00:04:19,120
as if just out of bed; and he had no good explanation to offer of his visit.

70
00:04:20,260 --> 00:04:24,832
Mainly on this evidence, though there was much more of a less striking and unusual

71
00:04:24,832 --> 00:04:29,460
kind from other parishioners, Mrs Mothersole was found guilty and condemned to die.

72
00:04:30,280 --> 00:04:35,600
She was hanged a week after the trial, with five or six more unhappy creatures,

73
00:04:35,600 --> 00:04:36,880
at Bury St Edmunds.

74
00:04:36,880 --> 00:04:41,060
Sir Matthew Fell, then Deputy-Sheriff, was present at the execution.

75
00:04:42,060 --> 00:04:46,173
It was a damp, drizzly March morning when the cart made its way up the rough grass

76
00:04:46,173 --> 00:04:48,580
hill outside Northgate, where the gallows stood.

77
00:04:49,460 --> 00:04:54,687
The other victims were apathetic or broken down with misery; but Mrs Mothersole was,

78
00:04:54,687 --> 00:04:57,860
as in life so in death, of a very different temper.

79
00:04:58,840 --> 00:05:02,016
Her “poysonous Rage”, as a reporter of the time puts it,

80
00:05:02,016 --> 00:05:04,114
“did so work upon the Bystanders—yea,

81
00:05:04,114 --> 00:05:08,765
even upon the Hangman—that it was constantly affirmed of all that saw her that she

82
00:05:08,765 --> 00:05:11,260
presented the living Aspect of a mad Divell.

83
00:05:11,260 --> 00:05:14,430
Yet she offer’d no Resistance to the Officers of the Law;

84
00:05:14,430 --> 00:05:18,992
onely she looked upon those that laid Hands upon her with so direfull and venomous

85
00:05:18,992 --> 00:05:23,497
an Aspect that—as one of them afterwards assured me—the meer Thought of it preyed

86
00:05:23,497 --> 00:05:26,000
inwardly upon his Mind for six Months after.”

87
00:05:27,260 --> 00:05:31,843
However, all that she is reported to have said was the seemingly meaningless words:

88
00:05:31,843 --> 00:05:33,720
“There will be guests at the Hall.

89
00:05:34,440 --> 00:05:36,780
Which she repeated more than once in an undertone.

90
00:05:37,760 --> 00:05:40,740
Sir Matthew Fell was not unimpressed by the bearing of the woman.

91
00:05:41,620 --> 00:05:44,646
He had some talk upon the matter with the Vicar of his parish,

92
00:05:44,646 --> 00:05:47,720
with whom he travelled home after the assize business was over.

93
00:05:48,640 --> 00:05:51,604
His evidence at the trial had not been very willingly given;

94
00:05:51,604 --> 00:05:55,359
he was not specially infected with the witch-finding mania, but he declared,

95
00:05:55,359 --> 00:05:59,311
then and afterwards, that he could not give any other account of the matter than

96
00:05:59,311 --> 00:06:03,362
that he had given, and that he could not possibly have been mistaken as to what he

97
00:06:03,362 --> 00:06:03,962
saw.

98
00:06:04,540 --> 00:06:07,017
The whole transaction had been repugnant to him,

99
00:06:07,017 --> 00:06:10,734
for he was a man who liked to be on pleasant terms with those about him;

100
00:06:10,734 --> 00:06:14,140
but he saw a duty to be done in this business, and he had done it.

101
00:06:14,880 --> 00:06:19,021
That seems to have been the gist of his sentiments, and the Vicar applauded it,

102
00:06:19,021 --> 00:06:20,960
as any reasonable man must have done.

103
00:06:22,800 --> 00:06:25,959
A few weeks after, when the moon of May was at the full,

104
00:06:25,959 --> 00:06:30,020
Vicar and Squire met again in the park, and walked to the Hall together.

105
00:06:31,280 --> 00:06:34,567
Lady Fell was with her mother, who was dangerously ill,

106
00:06:34,567 --> 00:06:38,033
and Sir Matthew was alone at home; so the Vicar, Mr Crome,

107
00:06:38,033 --> 00:06:41,320
was easily persuaded to take a late supper at the Hall.

108
00:06:42,500 --> 00:06:45,000
Sir Matthew was not very good company this evening.

109
00:06:45,880 --> 00:06:50,210
The talk ran chiefly on family and parish matters, and, as luck would have it,

110
00:06:50,210 --> 00:06:54,596
Sir Matthew made a memorandum in writing of certain wishes or intentions of his

111
00:06:54,596 --> 00:06:58,260
regarding his estates, which afterwards proved exceedingly useful.

112
00:06:59,360 --> 00:07:03,289
When Mr Crome thought of starting for home, about half past nine o’clock,

113
00:07:03,289 --> 00:07:07,757
Sir Matthew and he took a preliminary turn on the gravelled walk at the back of the

114
00:07:07,757 --> 00:07:08,357
house.

115
00:07:08,760 --> 00:07:13,026
The only incident that struck Mr Crome was this: they were in sight of the ash-tree

116
00:07:13,026 --> 00:07:16,213
which I described as growing near the windows of the building,

117
00:07:16,213 --> 00:07:17,960
when Sir Matthew stopped and said:

118
00:07:19,300 --> 00:07:22,200
“What is that that runs up and down the stem of the ash?

119
00:07:22,980 --> 00:07:23,960
It is never a squirrel?

120
00:07:24,760 --> 00:07:27,700
They will all be in their nests by now.”

121
00:07:28,260 --> 00:07:30,501
The Vicar looked and saw the moving creature,

122
00:07:30,501 --> 00:07:33,340
but he could make nothing of its colour in the moonlight.

123
00:07:34,220 --> 00:07:38,543
The sharp outline, however, seen for an instant, was imprinted on his brain,

124
00:07:38,543 --> 00:07:43,264
and he could have sworn, he said, though it sounded foolish, that, squirrel or not,

125
00:07:43,264 --> 00:07:44,800
it had more than four legs.

126
00:07:46,140 --> 00:07:50,900
Still, not much was to be made of the momentary vision, and the two men parted.

127
00:07:51,640 --> 00:07:54,680
They may have met since then, but it was not for a score of years.

128
00:07:55,680 --> 00:07:59,929
Next day Sir Matthew Fell was not downstairs at six in the morning,

129
00:07:59,929 --> 00:08:03,100
as was his custom, nor at seven, nor yet at eight.

130
00:08:04,160 --> 00:08:06,860
Hereupon the servants went and knocked at his chamber door.

131
00:08:07,920 --> 00:08:11,224
I need not prolong the description of their anxious listenings and renewed

132
00:08:11,224 --> 00:08:12,340
batterings on the panels.

133
00:08:13,160 --> 00:08:15,430
The door was opened at last from the outside,

134
00:08:15,430 --> 00:08:17,600
and they found their master dead and black.

135
00:08:18,540 --> 00:08:19,780
So much you have guessed.

136
00:08:20,660 --> 00:08:24,032
That there were any marks of violence did not at the moment appear;

137
00:08:24,032 --> 00:08:25,240
but the window was open.

138
00:08:26,560 --> 00:08:28,632
One of the men went to fetch the parson,

139
00:08:28,632 --> 00:08:32,000
and then by his directions rode on to give notice to the coroner.

140
00:08:32,840 --> 00:08:35,685
Mr Crome himself went as quick as he might to the Hall,

141
00:08:35,685 --> 00:08:38,220
and was shown to the room where the dead man lay.

142
00:08:39,060 --> 00:08:43,354
He has left some notes among his papers which show how genuine a respect and sorrow

143
00:08:43,354 --> 00:08:46,302
was felt for Sir Matthew, and there is also this passage,

144
00:08:46,302 --> 00:08:50,492
which I transcribe for the sake of the light it throws upon the course of events,

145
00:08:50,492 --> 00:08:52,820
and also upon the common beliefs of the time:

146
00:08:53,560 --> 00:08:57,795
“There was not any the least Trace of an Entrance having been forc’d to the Chamber:

147
00:08:57,795 --> 00:09:01,980
but the Casement stood open, as my poor Friend would always have it in this Season.

148
00:09:02,780 --> 00:09:07,214
He had his Evening Drink of small Ale in a silver vessel of about a pint measure,

149
00:09:07,214 --> 00:09:09,020
and tonight had not drunk it out.

150
00:09:09,680 --> 00:09:14,444
This Drink was examined by the Physician from Bury, a Mr Hodgkins, who could not,

151
00:09:14,444 --> 00:09:18,973
however, as he afterwards declar’d upon his Oath, before the Coroner’s quest,

152
00:09:18,973 --> 00:09:22,620
discover that any matter of a venomous kind was present in it.

153
00:09:23,340 --> 00:09:27,658
For, as was natural, in the great Swelling and Blackness of the Corpse,

154
00:09:27,658 --> 00:09:30,760
there was talk made among the Neighbours of Poyson.

155
00:09:30,760 --> 00:09:33,872
The Body was very much Disorder’d as it laid in the Bed,

156
00:09:33,872 --> 00:09:38,539
being twisted after so extream a sort as gave too probable Conjecture that my worthy

157
00:09:38,539 --> 00:09:41,540
Friend and Patron had expir’d in great Pain and Agony.

158
00:09:42,420 --> 00:09:46,428
And what is as yet unexplain’d, and to myself the Argument of some Horrid and

159
00:09:46,428 --> 00:09:50,177
Artfull Designe in the Perpetrators of this Barbarous Murther, was this,

160
00:09:50,177 --> 00:09:54,393
that the Women which were entrusted with the laying-out of the Corpse and washing

161
00:09:54,393 --> 00:09:58,610
it, being both sad Persons and very well Respected in their Mournfull Profession,

162
00:09:58,610 --> 00:10:02,254
came to me in a great Pain and Distress both of Mind and Body, saying,

163
00:10:02,254 --> 00:10:04,649
what was indeed confirmed upon the first View,

164
00:10:04,649 --> 00:10:09,022
that they had no sooner touch’d the Breast of the Corpse with their naked Hands than

165
00:10:09,022 --> 00:10:13,394
they were sensible of a more than ordinary violent Smart and Acheing in their Palms,

166
00:10:13,394 --> 00:10:17,247
which, with their whole Forearms, in no long time swell’d so immoderately,

167
00:10:17,247 --> 00:10:20,058
the Pain still continuing, that, as afterwards proved,

168
00:10:20,058 --> 00:10:23,962
during many weeks they were forc’d to lay by the exercise of their Calling;

169
00:10:23,962 --> 00:10:25,680
and yet no mark seen on the Skin.

170
00:10:26,820 --> 00:10:30,576
“Upon hearing this, I sent for the Physician, who was still in the House,

171
00:10:30,576 --> 00:10:34,743
and we made as carefull a Proof as we were able by the Help of a small Magnifying

172
00:10:34,743 --> 00:10:38,911
Lens of Crystal of the condition of the Skinn on this Part of the Body: but could

173
00:10:38,911 --> 00:10:43,078
not detect with the Instrument we had any Matter of Importance beyond a couple of

174
00:10:43,078 --> 00:10:47,143
small Punctures or Pricks, which we then concluded were the Spotts by which the

175
00:10:47,143 --> 00:10:50,487
Poyson might be introduced, remembering that Ring of Pope Borgia,

176
00:10:50,487 --> 00:10:54,654
with other known Specimens of the Horrid Art of the Italian Poysoners of the last

177
00:10:54,654 --> 00:10:55,254
age.

178
00:10:55,700 --> 00:10:58,400
“So much is to be said of the Symptoms seen on the Corpse.

179
00:10:59,260 --> 00:11:02,275
As to what I am to add, it is meerly my own Experiment,

180
00:11:02,275 --> 00:11:06,660
and to be left to Posterity to judge whether there be anything of Value therein.

181
00:11:07,440 --> 00:11:11,048
There was on the Table by the Beddside a Bible of the small size,

182
00:11:11,048 --> 00:11:14,268
in which my Friend—punctuall as in Matters of less Moment,

183
00:11:14,268 --> 00:11:18,043
so in this more weighty one—used nightly, and upon his First Rising,

184
00:11:18,043 --> 00:11:19,320
to read a sett Portion.

185
00:11:20,180 --> 00:11:24,651
And I taking it up—not without a Tear duly paid to him wich from the Study of this

186
00:11:24,651 --> 00:11:29,012
poorer Adumbration was now pass’d to the contemplation of its great Originall—it

187
00:11:29,012 --> 00:11:33,483
came into my Thoughts, as at such moments of Helplessness we are prone to catch at

188
00:11:33,483 --> 00:11:36,209
any the least Glimmer that makes promise of Light,

189
00:11:36,209 --> 00:11:40,626
to make trial of that old and by many accounted Superstitious Practice of drawing

190
00:11:40,626 --> 00:11:42,970
the Sortes; of which a Principall Instance,

191
00:11:42,970 --> 00:11:47,441
in the case of his late Sacred Majesty the Blessed Martyr King Charles and my Lord

192
00:11:47,441 --> 00:11:49,240
Falkland, was now much talked of.

193
00:11:50,080 --> 00:11:53,855
I must needs admit that by my Trial not much Assistance was afforded me: yet,

194
00:11:53,855 --> 00:11:57,777
as the Cause and Origin of these Dreadfull Events may hereafter be search’d out,

195
00:11:57,777 --> 00:12:01,601
I set down the Results, in the case it may be found that they pointed the true

196
00:12:01,601 --> 00:12:04,640
Quarter of the Mischief to a quicker Intelligence than my own.

197
00:12:06,440 --> 00:12:11,813
“I made, then, three trials, opening the Book and placing my Finger upon certain

198
00:12:11,813 --> 00:12:16,850
Words: which gave in the first these words, from Luke xiii. 7, Cut it down;

199
00:12:16,850 --> 00:12:20,880
in the second, Isaiah xiii. 20, It shall never be inhabited;

200
00:12:20,880 --> 00:12:26,320
and upon the third Experiment, Job xxxix. 30, Her young ones also suck up blood.”

201
00:12:27,480 --> 00:12:30,360
This is all that need be quoted from Mr Crome’s papers.

202
00:12:31,360 --> 00:12:35,665
Sir Matthew Fell was duly coffined and laid into the earth, and his funeral sermon,

203
00:12:35,665 --> 00:12:37,999
preached by Mr Crome on the following Sunday,

204
00:12:37,999 --> 00:12:41,215
has been printed under the title of “The Unsearchable Way; or,

205
00:12:41,215 --> 00:12:44,276
England’s Danger and the Malicious Dealings of Antichrist”,

206
00:12:44,276 --> 00:12:48,581
it being the Vicar’s view, as well as that most commonly held in the neighbourhood,

207
00:12:48,581 --> 00:12:52,160
that the Squire was the victim of a recrudescence of the Popish Plot.

208
00:12:53,240 --> 00:12:57,500
His son, Sir Matthew the second, succeeded to the title and estates.

209
00:12:58,340 --> 00:13:01,260
And so ends the first act of the Castringham tragedy.

210
00:13:02,260 --> 00:13:05,210
It is to be mentioned, though the fact is not surprising,

211
00:13:05,210 --> 00:13:09,040
that the new Baronet did not occupy the room in which his father had died.

212
00:13:09,740 --> 00:13:13,813
Nor, indeed, was it slept in by anyone but an occasional visitor during the whole of

213
00:13:13,813 --> 00:13:14,540
his occupation.

214
00:13:14,540 --> 00:13:19,494
He died in 1735, and I do not find that anything particular marked his reign,

215
00:13:19,494 --> 00:13:24,576
save a curiously constant mortality among his cattle and live-stock in general,

216
00:13:24,576 --> 00:13:28,500
which showed a tendency to increase slightly as time went on.

217
00:13:29,420 --> 00:13:33,924
Those who are interested in the details will find a statistical account in a letter

218
00:13:33,924 --> 00:13:35,878
to the Gentleman’s Magazine of 1772,

219
00:13:35,878 --> 00:13:38,700
which draws the facts from the Baronet’s own papers.

220
00:13:39,360 --> 00:13:42,598
He put an end to it at last by a very simple expedient,

221
00:13:42,598 --> 00:13:45,717
that of shutting up all his beasts in sheds at night,

222
00:13:45,717 --> 00:13:47,660
and keeping no sheep in his park.

223
00:13:48,240 --> 00:13:51,960
For he had noticed that nothing was ever attacked that spent the night indoors.

224
00:13:53,060 --> 00:13:57,500
After that the disorder confined itself to wild birds, and beasts of chase.

225
00:13:58,420 --> 00:14:00,732
But as we have no good account of the symptoms,

226
00:14:00,732 --> 00:14:03,732
and as all-night watching was quite unproductive of any clue,

227
00:14:03,732 --> 00:14:07,520
I do not dwell on what the Suffolk farmers called the “Castringham sickness”.

228
00:14:08,920 --> 00:14:14,608
The second Sir Matthew died in 1735, as I said, and was duly succeeded by his son,

229
00:14:14,608 --> 00:14:15,440
Sir Richard.

230
00:14:16,220 --> 00:14:20,293
It was in his time that the great family pew was built out on the north side of the

231
00:14:20,293 --> 00:14:20,980
parish church.

232
00:14:22,020 --> 00:14:26,345
So large were the Squire’s ideas that several of the graves on that unhallowed side

233
00:14:26,345 --> 00:14:29,680
of the building had to be disturbed to satisfy his requirements.

234
00:14:30,660 --> 00:14:35,559
Among them was that of Mrs Mothersole, the position of which was accurately known,

235
00:14:35,559 --> 00:14:39,920
thanks to a note on a plan of the church and yard, both made by Mr Crome.

236
00:14:40,580 --> 00:14:45,192
A certain amount of interest was excited in the village when it was known that the

237
00:14:45,192 --> 00:14:48,960
famous witch, who was still remembered by a few, was to be exhumed.

238
00:14:49,580 --> 00:14:52,792
And the feeling of surprise, and indeed disquiet,

239
00:14:52,792 --> 00:14:55,349
was very strong when it was found that,

240
00:14:55,349 --> 00:14:58,496
though her coffin was fairly sound and unbroken,

241
00:14:58,496 --> 00:15:02,560
there was no trace whatever inside it of body, bones, or dust.

242
00:15:02,560 --> 00:15:04,448
Indeed, it is a curious phenomenon,

243
00:15:04,448 --> 00:15:08,818
for at the time of her burying no such things were dreamt of as resurrection-men,

244
00:15:08,818 --> 00:15:13,188
and it is difficult to conceive any rational motive for stealing a body otherwise

245
00:15:13,188 --> 00:15:15,400
than for the uses of the dissecting-room.

246
00:15:16,460 --> 00:15:20,807
The incident revived for a time all the stories of witch-trials and of the exploits

247
00:15:20,807 --> 00:15:22,902
of the witches, dormant for forty years,

248
00:15:22,902 --> 00:15:27,302
and Sir Richard’s orders that the coffin should be burnt were thought by a good many

249
00:15:27,302 --> 00:15:30,340
to be rather foolhardy, though they were duly carried out.

250
00:15:31,080 --> 00:15:34,420
Sir Richard was a pestilent innovator, it is certain.

251
00:15:35,480 --> 00:15:39,164
Before his time the Hall had been a fine block of the mellowest red brick;

252
00:15:39,164 --> 00:15:43,246
but Sir Richard had travelled in Italy and become infected with the Italian taste,

253
00:15:43,246 --> 00:15:45,487
and, having more money than his predecessors,

254
00:15:45,487 --> 00:15:49,320
he determined to leave an Italian palace where he had found an English house.

255
00:15:50,340 --> 00:15:52,522
So stucco and ashlar masked the brick;

256
00:15:52,522 --> 00:15:57,287
some indifferent Roman marbles were planted about in the entrance-hall and gardens;

257
00:15:57,287 --> 00:16:01,995
a reproduction of the Sibyl’s temple at Tivoli was erected on the opposite bank of

258
00:16:01,995 --> 00:16:06,818
the mere; and Castringham took on an entirely new, and, I must say, a less engaging,

259
00:16:06,818 --> 00:16:07,418
aspect.

260
00:16:08,060 --> 00:16:12,401
But it was much admired, and served as a model to a good many of the neighbouring

261
00:16:12,401 --> 00:16:13,580
gentry in after-years.

262
00:16:14,680 --> 00:16:20,120
One morning (it was in 1754) Sir Richard woke after a night of discomfort.

263
00:16:20,900 --> 00:16:24,150
It had been windy, and his chimney had smoked persistently,

264
00:16:24,150 --> 00:16:26,960
and yet it was so cold that he must keep up a fire.

265
00:16:27,820 --> 00:16:31,426
Also something had so rattled about the window that no man could get a moment’s

266
00:16:31,426 --> 00:16:32,026
peace.

267
00:16:32,860 --> 00:16:37,388
Further, there was the prospect of several guests of position arriving in the course

268
00:16:37,388 --> 00:16:39,975
of the day, who would expect sport of some kind,

269
00:16:39,975 --> 00:16:44,503
and the inroads of the distemper (which continued among his game) had been lately so

270
00:16:44,503 --> 00:16:48,060
serious that he was afraid for his reputation as a game-preserver.

271
00:16:48,760 --> 00:16:52,700
But what really touched him most nearly was the other matter of his sleepless night.

272
00:16:53,520 --> 00:16:55,680
He could certainly not sleep in that room again.

273
00:16:56,540 --> 00:16:59,664
That was the chief subject of his meditations at breakfast,

274
00:16:59,664 --> 00:17:04,060
and after it he began a systematic examination of the rooms to see which would suit

275
00:17:04,060 --> 00:17:04,960
his notions best.

276
00:17:05,820 --> 00:17:07,480
It was long before he found one.

277
00:17:08,420 --> 00:17:11,806
This had a window with an eastern aspect and that with a northern;

278
00:17:11,806 --> 00:17:14,217
this door the servants would be always passing,

279
00:17:14,217 --> 00:17:16,320
and he did not like the bedstead in that.

280
00:17:17,180 --> 00:17:19,723
No, he must have a room with a western look-out,

281
00:17:19,723 --> 00:17:21,895
so that the sun could not wake him early,

282
00:17:21,895 --> 00:17:25,020
and it must be out of the way of the business of the house.

283
00:17:25,880 --> 00:17:27,920
The housekeeper was at the end of her resources.

284
00:17:29,880 --> 00:17:34,283
“Well, Sir Richard,” she said, “you know that there is but the one room like that in

285
00:17:34,283 --> 00:17:34,883
the house.”

286
00:17:36,220 --> 00:17:38,620
“Which may that be?” said Sir Richard.

287
00:17:39,640 --> 00:17:41,960
“And that is Sir Matthew’s—the West Chamber.”

288
00:17:43,180 --> 00:17:47,760
“Well, put me in there, for there I’ll lie tonight,” said her master.

289
00:17:48,600 --> 00:17:49,620
“Which way is it?

290
00:17:50,420 --> 00:17:52,620
Here, to be sure;” and he hurried off.

291
00:17:53,720 --> 00:17:57,420
“Oh, Sir Richard, but no one has slept there these forty years.

292
00:17:58,480 --> 00:18:01,600
The air has hardly been changed since Sir Matthew died there.”

293
00:18:02,860 --> 00:18:05,560
Thus she spoke, and rustled after him.

294
00:18:05,560 --> 00:18:09,620
“Come, open the door, Mrs Chiddock.

295
00:18:09,620 --> 00:18:12,140
I’ll see the chamber, at least.”

296
00:18:12,520 --> 00:18:16,400
So it was opened, and, indeed, the smell was very close and earthy.

297
00:18:17,260 --> 00:18:21,117
Sir Richard crossed to the window, and, impatiently, as was his wont,

298
00:18:21,117 --> 00:18:24,080
threw the shutters back, and flung open the casement.

299
00:18:24,820 --> 00:18:28,763
For this end of the house was one which the alterations had barely touched,

300
00:18:28,763 --> 00:18:33,180
grown up as it was with the great ash-tree, and being otherwise concealed from view.

301
00:18:33,180 --> 00:18:39,340
“Air it, Mrs Chiddock, all today, and move my bed-furniture in in the afternoon.

302
00:18:40,160 --> 00:18:42,420
Put the Bishop of Kilmore in my old room.”

303
00:18:43,620 --> 00:18:47,159
“Pray, Sir Richard,” said a new voice, breaking in on this speech,

304
00:18:47,159 --> 00:18:49,840
“might I have the favour of a moment’s interview?”

305
00:18:51,160 --> 00:18:55,080
Sir Richard turned round and saw a man in black in the doorway, who bowed.

306
00:18:56,120 --> 00:18:59,140
“I must ask your indulgence for this intrusion, Sir Richard.

307
00:18:59,840 --> 00:19:02,400
You will, perhaps, hardly remember me.

308
00:19:03,220 --> 00:19:08,266
My name is William Crome, and my grandfather was Vicar here in your grandfather’s

309
00:19:08,266 --> 00:19:08,640
time.”

310
00:19:08,640 --> 00:19:13,175
“Well, sir,” said Sir Richard, “the name of Crome is always a passport to

311
00:19:13,175 --> 00:19:13,920
Castringham.

312
00:19:14,500 --> 00:19:17,820
I am glad to renew a friendship of two generations” standing.

313
00:19:18,860 --> 00:19:23,844
In what can I serve you? for your hour of calling—and, if I do not mistake you,

314
00:19:23,844 --> 00:19:26,620
your bearing—shows you to be in some haste.”

315
00:19:27,080 --> 00:19:28,860
“That is no more than the truth, sir.

316
00:19:29,780 --> 00:19:33,180
I am riding from Norwich to Bury St Edmunds with what haste I can make,

317
00:19:33,180 --> 00:19:37,155
and I have called in on my way to leave with you some papers which we have but just

318
00:19:37,155 --> 00:19:40,220
come upon in looking over what my grandfather left at his death.

319
00:19:41,160 --> 00:19:43,920
It is thought you may find some matters of family interest in them.”

320
00:19:45,100 --> 00:19:47,202
“You are mighty obliging, Mr Crome, and,

321
00:19:47,202 --> 00:19:51,458
if you will be so good as to follow me to the parlour, and drink a glass of wine,

322
00:19:51,458 --> 00:19:54,400
we will take a first look at these same papers together.

323
00:19:54,400 --> 00:19:59,000
And you, Mrs Chiddock, as I said, be about airing this chamber….

324
00:19:59,840 --> 00:20:02,420
Yes, it is here my grandfather died….

325
00:20:03,260 --> 00:20:07,040
Yes, the tree, perhaps, does make the place a little dampish….

326
00:20:07,880 --> 00:20:10,640
No; I do not wish to listen to any more.

327
00:20:11,560 --> 00:20:12,980
Make no difficulties, I beg.

328
00:20:14,020 --> 00:20:15,140
You have your orders—go.

329
00:20:16,040 --> 00:20:17,180
Will you follow me, sir?”

330
00:20:18,080 --> 00:20:19,120
They went to the study.

331
00:20:19,800 --> 00:20:23,603
The packet which young Mr Crome had brought—he was then just become a Fellow of

332
00:20:23,603 --> 00:20:25,288
Clare Hall in Cambridge, I may say,

333
00:20:25,288 --> 00:20:29,091
and subsequently brought out a respectable edition of Polyaenus—contained among

334
00:20:29,091 --> 00:20:33,134
other things the notes which the old Vicar had made upon the occasion of Sir Matthew

335
00:20:33,134 --> 00:20:33,760
Fell’s death.

336
00:20:34,540 --> 00:20:38,564
And for the first time Sir Richard was confronted with the enigmatical Sortes

337
00:20:38,564 --> 00:20:40,080
Biblicæ which you have heard.

338
00:20:40,920 --> 00:20:42,180
They amused him a good deal.

339
00:20:43,500 --> 00:20:48,676
“Well,” he said, “my grandfather’s Bible gave one prudent piece of advice—Cut it

340
00:20:48,676 --> 00:20:49,276
down.

341
00:20:49,960 --> 00:20:54,000
If that stands for the ash-tree, he may rest assured I shall not neglect it.

342
00:20:54,840 --> 00:20:57,500
Such a nest of catarrhs and agues was never seen.”

343
00:20:58,820 --> 00:21:01,203
The parlour contained the family books, which,

344
00:21:01,203 --> 00:21:04,934
pending the arrival of a collection which Sir Richard had made in Italy,

345
00:21:04,934 --> 00:21:08,820
and the building of a proper room to receive them, were not many in number.

346
00:21:09,980 --> 00:21:12,460
Sir Richard looked up from the paper to the bookcase.

347
00:21:13,540 --> 00:21:17,340
“I wonder,” says he, “whether the old prophet is there yet?

348
00:21:18,240 --> 00:21:19,380
I fancy I see him.”

349
00:21:20,600 --> 00:21:25,070
Crossing the room, he took out a dumpy Bible, which, sure enough,

350
00:21:25,070 --> 00:21:30,640
bore on the flyleaf the inscription: “To Matthew Fell, from his Loving Godmother,

351
00:21:30,640 --> 00:21:32,840
Anne Aldous, 2 September, 1659.”

352
00:21:33,780 --> 00:21:36,780
“It would be no bad plan to test him again, Mr Crome.

353
00:21:37,560 --> 00:21:40,340
I will wager we get a couple of names in the Chronicles.

354
00:21:40,340 --> 00:21:43,040
H’m! what have we here?

355
00:21:44,020 --> 00:21:46,820
‘Thou shalt seek me in the morning, and I shall not be.

356
00:21:47,820 --> 00:21:48,520
Well, well!

357
00:21:49,600 --> 00:21:52,180
Your grandfather would have made a fine omen of that, hey?

358
00:21:53,080 --> 00:21:54,420
No more prophets for me!

359
00:21:55,280 --> 00:21:56,440
They are all in a tale.

360
00:21:57,400 --> 00:22:01,240
And now, Mr Crome, I am infinitely obliged to you for your packet.

361
00:22:02,120 --> 00:22:04,160
You will, I fear, be impatient to get on.

362
00:22:04,940 --> 00:22:07,600
Pray allow me—another glass.”

363
00:22:07,600 --> 00:22:12,142
So with offers of hospitality, which were genuinely meant (for Sir Richard thought

364
00:22:12,142 --> 00:22:15,300
well of the young man’s address and manner), they parted.

365
00:22:16,180 --> 00:22:21,217
In the afternoon came the guests—the Bishop of Kilmore, Lady Mary Hervey,

366
00:22:21,217 --> 00:22:23,080
Sir William Kentfield, etc.

367
00:22:24,100 --> 00:22:29,280
Dinner at five, wine, cards, supper, and dispersal to bed.

368
00:22:30,340 --> 00:22:34,060
Next morning Sir Richard is disinclined to take his gun with the rest.

369
00:22:35,020 --> 00:22:36,880
He talks with the Bishop of Kilmore.

370
00:22:37,820 --> 00:22:41,526
This prelate, unlike a good many of the Irish Bishops of his day,

371
00:22:41,526 --> 00:22:45,860
had visited his see, and, indeed, resided there, for some considerable time.

372
00:22:46,820 --> 00:22:50,503
This morning, as the two were walking along the terrace and talking over the

373
00:22:50,503 --> 00:22:53,362
alterations and improvements in the house, the Bishop said,

374
00:22:53,362 --> 00:22:55,300
pointing to the window of the West Room:

375
00:22:56,680 --> 00:23:00,340
“You could never get one of my Irish flock to occupy that room, Sir Richard.”

376
00:23:01,540 --> 00:23:02,780
“Why is that, my lord?

377
00:23:03,680 --> 00:23:05,180
It is, in fact, my own.”

378
00:23:06,120 --> 00:23:10,450
“Well, our Irish peasantry will always have it that it brings the worst of luck to

379
00:23:10,450 --> 00:23:14,728
sleep near an ash-tree, and you have a fine growth of ash not two yards from your

380
00:23:14,728 --> 00:23:15,520
chamber window.

381
00:23:16,460 --> 00:23:18,991
Perhaps,” the Bishop went on, with a smile,

382
00:23:18,991 --> 00:23:23,111
“it has given you a touch of its quality already, for you do not seem,

383
00:23:23,111 --> 00:23:27,819
if I may say it, so much the fresher for your night’s rest as your friends would

384
00:23:27,819 --> 00:23:28,820
like to see you.”

385
00:23:29,980 --> 00:23:36,160
“That, or something else, it is true, cost me my sleep from twelve to four, my lord.

386
00:23:36,960 --> 00:23:40,900
But the tree is to come down tomorrow, so I shall not hear much more from it.”

387
00:23:42,000 --> 00:23:43,520
“I applaud your determination.

388
00:23:44,600 --> 00:23:48,296
It can hardly be wholesome to have the air you breathe strained, as it were,

389
00:23:48,296 --> 00:23:49,560
through all that leafage.”

390
00:23:51,500 --> 00:23:53,380
“Your lordship is right there, I think.

391
00:23:54,280 --> 00:23:56,400
But I had not my window open last night.

392
00:23:57,220 --> 00:24:01,396
It was rather the noise that went on—no doubt from the twigs sweeping the glass—that

393
00:24:01,396 --> 00:24:02,340
kept me open-eyed.”

394
00:24:03,380 --> 00:24:05,420
“I think that can hardly be, Sir Richard.

395
00:24:06,420 --> 00:24:07,800
Here—you see it from this point.

396
00:24:08,740 --> 00:24:12,607
None of these nearest branches even can touch your casement unless there were a

397
00:24:12,607 --> 00:24:14,760
gale, and there was none of that last night.

398
00:24:15,340 --> 00:24:17,120
They miss the panes by a foot.”

399
00:24:18,280 --> 00:24:19,400
“No, sir, true.

400
00:24:20,480 --> 00:24:24,600
What, then, will it be, I wonder, that scratched and rustled so—ay,

401
00:24:24,600 --> 00:24:27,920
and covered the dust on my sill with lines and marks?”

402
00:24:29,100 --> 00:24:32,420
At last they agreed that the rats must have come up through the ivy.

403
00:24:33,300 --> 00:24:36,600
That was the Bishop’s idea, and Sir Richard jumped at it.

404
00:24:37,700 --> 00:24:42,538
So the day passed quietly, and night came, and the party dispersed to their rooms,

405
00:24:42,538 --> 00:24:44,780
and wished Sir Richard a better night.

406
00:24:45,820 --> 00:24:49,820
And now we are in his bedroom, with the light out and the Squire in bed.

407
00:24:50,720 --> 00:24:54,596
The room is over the kitchen, and the night outside still and warm,

408
00:24:54,596 --> 00:24:56,100
so the window stands open.

409
00:24:56,820 --> 00:24:59,230
There is very little light about the bedstead,

410
00:24:59,230 --> 00:25:01,220
but there is a strange movement there;

411
00:25:01,220 --> 00:25:05,410
it seems as if Sir Richard were moving his head rapidly to and fro with only the

412
00:25:05,410 --> 00:25:06,720
slightest possible sound.

413
00:25:07,280 --> 00:25:10,731
And now you would guess, so deceptive is the half-darkness,

414
00:25:10,731 --> 00:25:15,118
that he had several heads, round and brownish, which move back and forward,

415
00:25:15,118 --> 00:25:16,580
even as low as his chest.

416
00:25:17,460 --> 00:25:18,620
It is a horrible illusion.

417
00:25:19,620 --> 00:25:20,400
Is it nothing more?

418
00:25:21,160 --> 00:25:25,507
There! something drops off the bed with a soft plump, like a kitten,

419
00:25:25,507 --> 00:25:27,808
and is out of the window in a flash;

420
00:25:27,808 --> 00:25:30,940
another—four—and after that there is quiet again.

421
00:25:31,060 --> 00:25:35,380
“Thou shalt seek me in the morning, and I shall not be.”

422
00:25:36,760 --> 00:25:40,840
As with Sir Matthew, so with Sir Richard—dead and black in his bed!

423
00:25:42,180 --> 00:25:45,977
A pale and silent party of guests and servants gathered under the window when the

424
00:25:45,977 --> 00:25:46,680
news was known.

425
00:25:47,900 --> 00:25:49,995
Italian poisoners, Popish emissaries,

426
00:25:49,995 --> 00:25:53,052
infected air—all these and more guesses were hazarded,

427
00:25:53,052 --> 00:25:55,600
and the Bishop of Kilmore looked at the tree,

428
00:25:55,600 --> 00:25:59,223
in the fork of whose lower boughs a white tom-cat was crouching,

429
00:25:59,223 --> 00:26:02,620
looking down the hollow which years had gnawed in the trunk.

430
00:26:03,480 --> 00:26:06,160
It was watching something inside the tree with great interest.

431
00:26:07,580 --> 00:26:09,700
Suddenly it got up and craned over the hole.

432
00:26:10,660 --> 00:26:15,160
Then a bit of the edge on which it stood gave way, and it went slithering in.

433
00:26:16,000 --> 00:26:18,020
Everyone looked up at the noise of the fall.

434
00:26:19,020 --> 00:26:23,320
It is known to most of us that a cat can cry; but few of us have heard, I hope,

435
00:26:23,320 --> 00:26:26,260
such a yell as came out of the trunk of the great ash.

436
00:26:26,860 --> 00:26:31,288
Two or three screams there were—the witnesses are not sure which—and then a slight

437
00:26:31,288 --> 00:26:34,960
and muffled noise of some commotion or struggling was all that came.

438
00:26:36,000 --> 00:26:38,011
But Lady Mary Hervey fainted outright,

439
00:26:38,011 --> 00:26:41,980
and the housekeeper stopped her ears and fled till she fell on the terrace.

440
00:26:43,260 --> 00:26:46,240
The Bishop of Kilmore and Sir William Kentfield stayed.

441
00:26:47,280 --> 00:26:50,983
Yet even they were daunted, though it was only at the cry of a cat;

442
00:26:50,983 --> 00:26:54,300
and Sir William swallowed once or twice before he could say:

443
00:26:54,920 --> 00:26:58,340
“There is something more than we know of in that tree, my lord.

444
00:26:59,160 --> 00:27:00,680
I am for an instant search.”

445
00:27:01,780 --> 00:27:03,120
And this was agreed upon.

446
00:27:04,100 --> 00:27:07,080
A ladder was brought, and one of the gardeners went up, and,

447
00:27:07,080 --> 00:27:11,252
looking down the hollow, could detect nothing but a few dim indications of something

448
00:27:11,252 --> 00:27:11,852
moving.

449
00:27:12,620 --> 00:27:15,100
They got a lantern, and let it down by a rope.

450
00:27:16,280 --> 00:27:17,980
“We must get at the bottom of this.

451
00:27:18,600 --> 00:27:23,500
My life upon it, my lord, but the secret of these terrible deaths is there.”

452
00:27:24,400 --> 00:27:28,640
Up went the gardener again with the lantern, and let it down the hole cautiously.

453
00:27:29,480 --> 00:27:32,480
They saw the yellow light upon his face as he bent over,

454
00:27:32,480 --> 00:27:36,927
and saw his face struck with an incredulous terror and loathing before he cried out

455
00:27:36,927 --> 00:27:40,409
in a dreadful voice and fell back from the ladder—where, happily,

456
00:27:40,409 --> 00:27:44,320
he was caught by two of the men—letting the lantern fall inside the tree.

457
00:27:45,280 --> 00:27:49,920
He was in a dead faint, and it was some time before any word could be got from him.

458
00:27:50,780 --> 00:27:52,800
By then they had something else to look at.

459
00:27:53,600 --> 00:27:56,103
The lantern must have broken at the bottom,

460
00:27:56,103 --> 00:28:00,177
and the light in it caught upon dry leaves and rubbish that lay there,

461
00:28:00,177 --> 00:28:04,426
for in a few minutes a dense smoke began to come up, and then flame; and,

462
00:28:04,426 --> 00:28:06,580
to be short, the tree was in a blaze.

463
00:28:07,840 --> 00:28:10,358
The bystanders made a ring at some yards’ distance,

464
00:28:10,358 --> 00:28:14,357
and Sir William and the Bishop sent men to get what weapons and tools they could;

465
00:28:14,357 --> 00:28:18,356
for, clearly, whatever might be using the tree as its lair would be forced out by

466
00:28:18,356 --> 00:28:18,956
the fire.

467
00:28:19,940 --> 00:28:20,800
So it was.

468
00:28:21,820 --> 00:28:26,616
First, at the fork, they saw a round body covered with fire—the size of a man’s

469
00:28:26,616 --> 00:28:30,440
head—appear very suddenly, then seem to collapse and fall back.

470
00:28:31,440 --> 00:28:36,562
This, five or six times; then a similar ball leapt into the air and fell on the

471
00:28:36,562 --> 00:28:39,220
grass, where after a moment it lay still.

472
00:28:40,100 --> 00:28:42,531
The Bishop went as near as he dared to it,

473
00:28:42,531 --> 00:28:46,640
and saw—what but the remains of an enormous spider, veinous and seared!

474
00:28:47,480 --> 00:28:49,203
And, as the fire burned lower down,

475
00:28:49,203 --> 00:28:52,404
more terrible bodies like this began to break out from the trunk,

476
00:28:52,404 --> 00:28:55,260
and it was seen that these were covered with greyish hair.

477
00:28:56,520 --> 00:29:00,710
All that day the ash burned, and until it fell to pieces the men stood about it,

478
00:29:00,710 --> 00:29:03,800
and from time to time killed the brutes as they darted out.

479
00:29:04,700 --> 00:29:07,359
At last there was a long interval when none appeared,

480
00:29:07,359 --> 00:29:10,620
and they cautiously closed in and examined the roots of the tree.

481
00:29:11,540 --> 00:29:13,736
“They found,” says the Bishop of Kilmore,

482
00:29:13,736 --> 00:29:16,199
“below it a rounded hollow place in the earth,

483
00:29:16,199 --> 00:29:20,644
wherein were two or three bodies of these creatures that had plainly been smothered

484
00:29:20,644 --> 00:29:24,446
by the smoke; and, what is to me more curious, at the side of this den,

485
00:29:24,446 --> 00:29:28,356
against the wall, was crouching the anatomy or skeleton of a human being,

486
00:29:28,356 --> 00:29:32,105
with the skin dried upon the bones, having some remains of black hair,

487
00:29:32,105 --> 00:29:36,282
which was pronounced by those that examined it to be undoubtedly the body of a

488
00:29:36,282 --> 00:29:39,120
woman, and clearly dead for a period of fifty years.”

